   "date": "2026-11-23",
   "html": "<p><strong>Reward forgery found and blocking GRPO; A2 amendment 1 registered; quick reviews 12:14, 12:19 and 12:24 triaged.</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Reward forgery (C83):</strong> <code>experiments/checks/reward_forgery_probe.py</code> (output in <code>results/reward_forgery_probe_2026-11-23.txt</code>) shows that solution code which sets <code>builtins.exec</code> or <code>builtins.compile</code> to a no-op scores <strong>1/1 on a failing test</strong>, in both the committed harness and the working tree. The honest control scores 0/1. Swallowing AssertionError and <code>os._exit(0)</code> do not forge.</li><li>C83 gains this evidence and a limitation.</li><li>The fix is SQ373: high priority, owned by the side-quest worker. Bind the real exec/compile before the solution runs, use a fresh namespace per test and an unreachable reporting channel, with the probe cases as must-score-0 tests.</li><li><strong>GRPO stays blocked until SQ373 passes.</strong> <code>evals/sandbox.py</code> uses the same harness, so exec-based pass@k is forgeable in principle. No model has been trained against this reward, so existing pass@k numbers are not affected.</li><li><strong>A2 amendment 1</strong> (registered before these runs): A1e (ternary core only) is confirmed or rejected on fresh seeds 2-4, paired with the E27 references already in the A2 wave. Prediction -6% (range -9% to -3%), shrunk for winner's curse. A3's leave-one-out contrasts will be Holm-adjusted as one family.</li><li><strong>First A2 numbers</strong> (not yet analysed; registered analysis when the wave completes): A2f seed 2, the full-precision assembled GDN + loops model, scores clean80 0.2501. Dense E20 seed 2 scores 0.2506 and E24 seed 2 scores 0.2412.</li><li><strong>Quick reviews:</strong> 12:14 (newcomer) gives a glossary and cold-start check task for the next docs refresh. 12:19 (safety) is the forgery above. 12:24 (statistician) is the A1e confirmation and the Holm rule for A3.</li></ul>"
