   "date": "2026-11-23",
   "html": "<p><strong>SQ263, the registered 200M gate on the five F2 cells</strong>, is analysed (24 runs on code-py-v2, 3 seeds per arm; cell, LR and tokens read from run <code>meta.json</code>; <code>experiments/checks/sq263.py</code>).</p><p>| Cell | 200M clean80, vs dense at each cell's best LR | |---|---| | 100 GDN | <strong>−4.0%</strong> | | 010 loops | +10.6% | | 001 ternary | +15.8% | | 111 full fusion | <strong>+30.8%</strong> |</p><ul><li><strong>Interaction:</strong> I = <strong>+6.21%</strong>, seed-blocked 95% CI [+4.20%, +8.22%], df 8 (Welch: +6.21%, [+4.44%, +7.97%]). <strong>Anti-synergy:</strong> at the registered horizon, the ingredients' penalties compound.</li><li><strong>H2' FAIL:</strong> the 20M-selected 6e-3 still beats 2e-3 at 200M in all three six-e-3 cells (2e-3 is worse by +0.93%, +0.20% and +0.64%). The reversal is not an LR-transfer artifact.</li><li><strong>C201 is REFUTED</strong> by its own registered refutation criterion. Its 20M synergy (−3.5%) is real at 20M only; the interaction's sign depends on the horizon.</li><li><strong>New claim C202:</strong> anti-synergy at 200M. It is PARTIAL, because H3' was registered as descriptive and there are 3 seeds per cell.</li><li><strong>What this means for the thesis:</strong> the full fusion is the worst cell at 200M. GDN alone remains the only ingredient that helps. The README and the F2 devlog are updated.</li><li><strong>Analysis timing:</strong> this was analysed in the session after the human's \"kill the fleet\"; the data had been collected before.</li></ul><p><strong>K9 reproduced exactly</strong> (quick review 08:24): a re-run at clean commit <code>d10f5a2</code> (CPU, 58 s) gives identical values for every measured field, including 1,667/4,000 near-duplicates and all per-doc overlaps. <code>K9.json</code> now records the clean commit, and C191's provenance limitation is superseded.</p><p><strong>Queue reconciled:</strong> E9, Q10, Q11 and Q12 are marked done (all ran in Phase 2b on the N1X or the 5090). The rendered next picks are now SQ50 (GPU) and SQ61 (CPU).</p><p><strong>Fleet killed</strong> (the human: \"kill the fleet\"):</p><ul><li>The last three boxes (w4-3, w4-4, w4-6) were pulled and destroyed at 08:24 EST. All E60 runs were saved: 18 at 200M plus the 9-run LR grid.</li><li><strong>Disclosure:</strong></li><li>the boxes sat idle for about 5 h after finishing (03:15–03:22). The pull-and-destroy watcher ran inside the agent session and stopped when the session was interrupted. Cost: about $8.5.</li><li><strong>Total compute spend is $30.13</strong>, against the approved $30 (credit $69.82).</li><li>Lesson saved to agent memory: destroy each box as its last job is pulled, and check instances at the start of every session.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick reviews 08:24 (reproducibility) and 08:29 (competitor lab):</strong></p><ul><li>SQ274 gains a commit-provenance check.</li><li>SQ335: the per-document spread of the F2 interaction.</li></ul>"
