   "version": "v0.4.100",
   "title": "Eval sandbox: /var/tmp leak found and fixed (12/12 self-test); paired batches verified; E60 LR 6e-3 for all arms",
   "date": "2026-11-23",
   "html": "<p><strong>Eval sandbox fix</strong> (quick review 02:54, safety):</p><ul><li><strong>The leak:</strong> a canary written to <code>/var/tmp</code> from inside <code>evals.sandbox.run_tests</code> persisted on the host, so one eval run could leave files for a later one. <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/dev/shm</code>, <code>/run</code> and <code>/mnt</code> were already private.</li><li><strong>The fix:</strong></li><li><code>/var/tmp</code> joins the private tmpfs mounts;</li><li>a new self-test probe, <code>shared_tmp_private</code>, checks that a write inside does not reach the host.</li><li><strong>Result:</strong></li><li>the re-probe leaves no canary on the host;</li><li><code>python -m evals.sandbox</code> passes <strong>12/12</strong> (<code>experiments/checks/results/test-runs/2026-11-23-sandbox-selftest.json</code>);</li><li>C106 gets the evidence.</li><li><strong>SQ61's gates now include:</strong> a world-writable-path sweep and resource budgets (02:39), alongside the network, filesystem and descendant-cleanup gates.</li></ul><p><strong>Paired batches verified empirically</strong> (quick review 02:44, data engineer):</p><ul><li><strong>The check:</strong> <code>experiments/checks/check_paired_batches.py</code> hashes the token ids for 20 steps under seeds 0 and 1. It compares two arms that use very different amounts of global RNG.</li><li><strong>The result:</strong> the hashes are identical at every step, and different seeds differ. The loader's own generator (<code>gogeta/train.py:61</code>) makes every seed-paired comparison truly paired.</li></ul><p><strong>E60 LR grid</strong> (registered G3 rule, clean80 at 20M):</p><p>| Arm | lr 3e-3 | lr 6e-3 | lr 1.2e-2 | |---|---|---|---| | E60a | 0.4493 | <strong>0.4423</strong> | 0.4574 | | E60b | 0.4507 | <strong>0.4434</strong> | 0.4663 | | E60c | 0.4474 | <strong>0.4438</strong> | 0.4795 |</p><ul><li>All three arms take <strong>6e-3</strong>, an interior minimum, so no extension point is needed.</li><li>The 18 runs at 200M (3 arms × 6 seeds) are running on three 5090s.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick reviews 02:39, 02:44 and 02:54:</strong> triaged in their files.</p><p><strong>Fleet:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>w4-1 is destroyed</strong> (the human: \"tear down any vps … if we don't use it\").</li><li>A pull-and-destroy watcher will take down w4-3, w4-4 and w4-6 when E60 finishes. Nothing more is queued.</li><li><strong>Near-miss, disclosed:</strong> the watcher's first version called the destroy helper under Windows PowerShell 5.1. There, <code>ConvertFrom-Json</code> does not enumerate arrays, so the label filter matched every instance and the destroy received all four ids.</li><li>The Vast CLI rejected the multi-id call, and no box was lost (all four were verified running).</li><li>The helper now force-enumerates, requires exactly one exact-label match and refuses otherwise. It is dry-run-checked under PowerShell 5.1 and 7.</li></ul>"
