   "html": "<p><strong>Phase 2b-5090 is complete</strong> (<code>experiments/devlogs/PHASE2B-5090.md</code>, <code>experiments/checks/results/phase2b_5090.json</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Design:</strong> registered before dispatch (v0.4.74). 19 runs at 200M tokens on 6 rented RTX 5090s, commit <code>8441ad0</code>, each box's dataset rebuilt and verified byte-identical. Every checkpoint is scored on clean80.</li><li><strong>Clean80 bpc means:</strong></li><li>E10: 0.26772 (7 seeds);</li><li>E1: 0.26795 (7 seeds);</li><li>E11: 0.26710 (3 seeds);</li><li>E9: 0.2773 (1 seed);</li><li>E2: 0.2845 (1 seed).</li><li><strong>R1, E1 vs E10:</strong> E1 +0.08%, paired 95% CI [−0.00087, +0.00132], p 0.64. <strong>A tie.</strong> The sign matches the N1X test (C5 REFUTED, v0.4.78), but the CI spans 0. Over both machines (10 seeds per arm), E1 is within about ±0.4% of a tuned transformer and not better.</li><li><strong>R3, E11 vs E1:</strong> −0.13%, TOST p 0.002 inside the registered ±1% margin, so they are <strong>equivalent</strong>. New claim <strong>C196, VERIFIED</strong> (3 seeds, clean80, RTX 5090).</li><li><strong>Holm (m = 2):</strong> neither comparison rejects equality.</li><li><strong>R2, cross-SKU:</strong> <strong>pass</strong>. 6/6 matched items within 0.0010 val_loss of the N1X; clean80 of N1X vs 5090 checkpoints within 0.0008. C195 gets evidence; it stays PARTIAL because the 3090 half is 20M only.</li><li><strong>Loops (descriptive, 1 seed):</strong> E2 (looped) is 2.6% worse than its unlooped twin E9 and 6.2% worse than E1. C6 gets evidence.</li></ul><p><strong>SQ232, same-SKU replay floor</strong> (<code>sq_232.json</code>):</p><ul><li>Same-seed 5090 replays differ by 0.0022 (E10) and 0.0028 (E1) at 20M tokens. P-R1 (≤ 0.002) fails narrowly.</li><li>So cross-SKU deltas of up to about 0.002 are within run-to-run noise (C195 limitation).</li></ul><p><strong>Registry:</strong> E10, E11 and E9 are now <code>done</code> in <code>experiments/experiments.json</code>, with their clean80 results; E1 gets its Phase 2b numbers. This fixes the stale \"E10 planned\" in INDEX that the reviews flagged.</p><p><strong>What this means:</strong> at 13.8M params and 200M tokens, the hybrid mixers (E1) tie a well-tuned transformer, and negative-eigenvalue GDN (E11) changes nothing measurable. The open questions move to:</p><ul><li>scale (SQ243, the two-size check);</li><li>implementation parity of the control (SQ234);</li><li>real wall-clock efficiency (SQ244);</li><li>whether loops or ternary can earn their place: the A3 factorial and controls now running on the fleet.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick review 21:47 (newcomer):</strong> SQ246 (calibration, descriptive) accepted. The main open test is now the FusionDance combination vs its controls.</p>"
