   "title": "Cloud pilot results: the 5090 is fastest and cheapest per token; SQ214 window sweep (w256 best); cockpit Vast integration; thermal-kill fixes",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p><strong>SQ220 done: cloud bridge across three machines</strong> (<code>experiments/devlogs/SQ220.md</code>, <code>experiments/checks/results/sq_220.json</code>).</p><ul><li><strong>Setup:</strong> the same training code (<code>gogeta/</code>, <code>configs/</code>, <code>tools/</code> identical at <code>9712842</code> and <code>bd03492</code>), the same data sha256, seed 0, lr 3e-3, 20M tokens.</li><li><strong>Loss transfers.</strong> val_loss vs the N1X:</li><li>E10: 3090 +0.0020, 5090 +0.0011;</li><li>E1: 3090 +0.0007, 5090 −0.0020.</li></ul><p>All are within the ±0.004 tolerance. New claim <strong>C195</strong> (PARTIAL: seed 0, 20M tokens, two SKUs). E1 &lt; E10 on every machine.</p><ul><li><strong>Speed (steady-state median tokens/s):</strong></li></ul><p>| | N1X | RTX 3090 | RTX 5090 | |---|---|---|---| | E10 | 202k | 272.5k | <strong>845k</strong> | | E1 | 138k | 254.5k | <strong>743k</strong> | | $/hr | ~0.02 (electricity, assumed) | 0.232 | 0.523 | | $ per 1B E1 tokens | ~0.04 | 0.253 | <strong>0.196</strong> | | 200M-token E1 run | 24.2 min | 13.1 min | <strong>4.5 min</strong> |</p><ul><li><strong>The 5090 wins both value per dollar</strong> (23–27% cheaper per token than the 3090) <strong>and time</strong> (about 3× the 3090). The 3090-part \"overhead-bound\" reading was wrong.</li><li><strong>Prediction outcomes:</strong> P3 was a partial miss (3090 only 1.35×/1.84×). 5-P2 and 5-P3 passed.</li><li><strong>Per-box fixed cost</strong> is about 3.3 min: 76 s env from scratch, about 90 s on-box dataset rebuild, about 30 s cold compile. Nothing is uploaded from the operator's machine.</li><li><strong>Spend:</strong> about $0.35 in total. Both boxes were destroyed as soon as their work ended (AGENTS §0h).</li></ul><p><strong>SQ214 done (window sweep part; exploratory)</strong> (<code>experiments/devlogs/SQ214.md</code>, <code>experiments/checks/results/sq_214.json</code>):</p><ul><li><strong>Design:</strong> E1's two SWA layers at window 64/256/1024, 4 seeds each, 20M tokens, one RTX 5090.</li><li><strong>Clean80 bpc means:</strong> w64 0.4509, <strong>w256 0.4475</strong>, w1024 0.4533.</li><li><strong>Full context is worse:</strong> +1.30% (paired t 5.1, df 3, all four seeds the same sign). w64 is +0.76%.</li><li><strong>Registered predictions:</strong> W1 fail, W2 pass, W3 fail. The decision rule says no confirmatory run.</li><li><strong>C193</strong> gets a limitation (event 4): the \"SWA window caps long-doc context\" explanation of its &gt; 1,200-token reversal loses support. SQ226 (scorer chunking) and SQ219 (context removal) are next.</li><li>The 20M-token seed sd is 0.0017 bpc (0.39%), a by-product for SQ216.</li></ul><p><strong>Incident: N1X thermal kill, then an unwanted auto-resume</strong> (post-mortem):</p><ul><li><strong>Cause:</strong></li><li>The N1X reached 86.6 °C (limit 85 °C) during Phase 2b item 7 (E11 s0), and the human ordered a kill. The campaign runner and the run were SIGTERMed; the partial E11 run is discarded.</li><li>The watchdog classified the runner's exit 143 as \"transient\" and auto-launched <code>phase2b-replication-resume-1</code>. E1 s0 ran on the N1X from 20:53 until a session/WSL restart killed it around 21:01.</li><li>Separately, the launcher only gates <em>new</em> launches on temperature; nothing throttles a running job.</li><li><strong>Fix:</strong></li><li>Exit 143/130 is classified <code>operator</code> and never auto-resumed.</li><li>A new operator hold (<code>cockpit.sh --hold</code>, human-only <code>--release-hold</code>) blocks every resume, and the TUI header shows it.</li><li>Two regression tests were added (watchdog 24/24).</li><li><strong>Prevention:</strong></li><li>The hold is set now, per the human's \"Hold for now\". Phase 2b stays at 6 of 11 until the human releases it.</li><li>A running-job thermal guard is queued.</li></ul><p><strong>Cockpit Vast integration</strong> (cockpit subagent, verified by the main agent: 77/77 tests, MCP probe, headless TUI check):</p><ul><li><strong>Plugins:</strong> a provider interface with Vast plus a bring-your-own SSH box.</li><li><strong>57 MCP tools:</strong> read-only status/offers/cost; confirm-gated setup/data/run/pull/down; budget-gated up/session/dispatch.</li><li><strong>Guards:</strong> spend checked before every rent under a human-only approval (<code>cockpit.sh --approve-spend</code>, TTY). AGENTS §0h is enforced in code: pull then destroy on an empty queue, a 10-min idle kill, teardown in <code>finally</code>.</li><li><strong>TUI:</strong> Cloud (<code>k</code>) and Fleet (<code>j</code>) tabs. The Fleet tab now polls live on open and every 15 s; it used to poll only on its Refresh button.</li><li><strong>Launcher:</strong> the <code>cockpit.ps1</code> wslpath bug is fixed.</li></ul><p><strong>Reconnection</strong> (the human: \"make sure the reconnection is smooth and easy to do if not automatic\"):</p><ul><li><code>cockpit.sh --reconnect</code> restarts the watchdog if it is down, then prints the hold, GPU, campaigns, incidents and live cloud instances.</li><li>Opening the TUI also starts the watchdog. After the restart the watchdog had been down, and nothing auto-started it.</li><li>AGENTS §0f now makes reconnecting the first step after any restart, and records the stop-means-stopped rule.</li></ul><p><strong>Quick review 20:50 (systems/hardware):</strong> SQ231 accepted (a micro-batch throughput curve with gradient accumulation, which keeps the optimisation identical).</p>"
