   "version": "v0.4.63",
   "title": "SQ99: E1's advantage does not transfer out of corpus (exploratory)",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p>### SQ99: out-of-corpus transfer (registered v0.4.54)</p><p><strong>What</strong>: <code>experiments/checks/sq_99_out_of_corpus.py</code> on the CPU (26 min, fp32, E1's GDN via the plain-PyTorch reference). It scored the v0.3.0 E0 and E1 seed-0 checkpoints on <strong>330 local Python files</strong> (stdlib + installed packages; mean 13-gram overlap with train 0.31%; 400k tokens) and on a <strong>token-matched sample of 1,340 val_clean80 docs</strong>, both through the same scorer.</p><p><strong>Results</strong> (<code>experiments/checks/results/sq_99.json</code>):</p><ul><li>Out-of-corpus bits/char is <strong>6.3–6.5×</strong> the in-corpus value for both models (format plus content shift).</li><li><strong>The E1-vs-E0 gap is +0.42% out of corpus (E1 worse; 95% paired BCa CI +0.06% to +0.78%), vs −3.44% in corpus (CI −3.82% to −2.88%).</strong></li><li><strong>Verdict: DOES NOT TRANSFER.</strong> Prediction (2), that E1 still wins out of corpus by less, failed.</li></ul><p><strong>Interpretation (cautious)</strong>:</p><ul><li>The hybrid's in-corpus advantage may be specific to this corpus: memorising its repeated templates (K9, A1 families).</li><li><strong>But</strong> this is exploratory: single-seed checkpoints at an untuned LR, and <strong>E0 may be a handicapped baseline</strong> (its scaled residual init differs from Llama's, SQ200). The handicap would act on both sets, so it's unclear how it moves the gap. Out-of-corpus is also confounded with document format.</li><li><strong>The decisive re-run</strong> uses the Phase 2b checkpoints (E10 as the baseline, E1 at its tuned LR, 3 seeds).</li></ul><p><strong>Ledger</strong>: new claim <strong>C192</strong> (\"E1's lower loss transfers to out-of-corpus Python\") is <strong>REFUTED</strong> [exploratory n=1]; a limitation was added to <strong>C5</strong>. Card <code>experiments/cards/SQ99.md</code>, micro devlog <code>experiments/devlogs/SQ99.md</code>. README status carries the warning.</p>"
