   "html": "<p>### Byte-level literature, 2025–2026 (the human's request)</p><p><strong>What</strong>: refs section <strong><code>14-byte-level</code></strong> in <code>refs/fetch_refs.py</code> holds <strong>32 papers, each verified against the arXiv API</strong> (title and date match): 8 foundational (ByT5, CANINE, Charformer, MegaByte, MambaByte, SpaceByte, BLT, bGPT) and 24 from 2025–26. 32 PDFs were downloaded (33.2 MB, git-ignored); <code>refs/README.md</code> was regenerated. The survey and its impact on SQ180–SQ189 are in <code>notes/research-byte-level-2025-26.md</code>. Numbers are from abstracts and HTML summaries, labelled \"reported\".</p><p><strong>Design changes folded into the side quests</strong>:</p><ul><li>per-position loss within each patch (patch lag);</li><li>a patch sweep up to 16;</li><li>fixed-shape top-K dynamic boundaries (ByteFlow Net);</li><li><strong>bytes are expected to lose at our scale</strong> (the tokenizer leads below ~4B on code), with a cheapest rescue arm.</li></ul><p>Also: bpb isn't tokenizer-agnostic for raw training loss, and entropy patching has a blind spot at our scale. No paper combines ternary weights with bytes. ### Quick review 18:57 (ML theorist, Codex GPT-6 Luna)</p><p><strong>SQ195</strong>, the loop-map Jacobian gain: power iterations on E2 activations at r = 4/8. Norm growth (K6) alone doesn't show local instability. It informs the loop fixes but doesn't hard-gate them.</p>"
