   "title": "A1 data integrity (final): clean-val lists, frozen test split, families, strict benchmark contamination",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p>### A1 data integrity (rigor workstream, final hand-back; snapshot was v0.4.46)</p><p><strong>What</strong>: <code>experiments/data_integrity/</code>:</p><ul><li>MinHash/LSH + substring dedup;</li><li>corpus family clustering;</li><li>a frozen test-split builder with an access guard;</li><li>strict benchmark contamination;</li><li>dataset, clean-val and test-split manifests.</li></ul><p>The method and policy are in <code>notes/data-integrity.md</code>. Test data lives only on the WSL disk.</p><p><strong>Results</strong> (reported by the subagent; the main agent re-checked the manifests):</p><ul><li><strong>The dedup tool works:</strong> planted-duplicate precision 1.00; recall 1.00 for exact and reformatted copies, 0.95 for a 60% prefix, 0.85–1.00 for renamed identifiers.</li><li><strong>No unused Python docs exist</strong> (all 991,154 are in train or val), and the corpus has <strong>no source/repo id</strong> (columns: type, lang, text). A first-function-name proxy: 93.5% of val docs share a name with a train doc.</li><li><strong>Families</strong> (MinHash, Jaccard ≥ 0.5): 27.1% of docs and 19.6% of tokens are in families of size ≥ 2; 741,056 families, the largest with 5,095 docs; 555 families span train and val. Redundancy is mostly templates, not copies.</li><li><strong>Clean val</strong> (<code>clean-val.manifest.json</code>, built from K9): <code>val_clean80</code> = 2,333 docs (the primary set for claims) and <code>val_clean50</code> = 1,174 docs (the strict sensitivity set).</li><li><strong>Strict benchmark contamination</strong> (vs the old C103 screen): <strong>HumanEval 74/164</strong> (C103 said 32), HumanEval+ 78/164, <strong>MBPP 103/500</strong> (C103 said 43), MBPP+ 65/378, CRUXEval 9/800, fresh GitHub 1/500. <strong>All earlier code-benchmark numbers should be treated as contaminated.</strong></li><li><strong>Frozen test split <code>code-py-v1-test</code>:</strong> 462 UncheatableEval 2026-10 files, 1,000,269 tokens, max 13-gram overlap with train/val 0.060, no family link; <code>--verify</code> passes. <code>code-py-v1-test-indist</code> (3,062 train docs) is valid only for a retrain that excludes them.</li><li><strong>A deduplicated v2</strong> (one doc per family) would keep 737.6k docs / 214.7M tokens (74.7% of docs, 81.8% of tokens).</li><li>The main agent's re-run of <code>build_dataset_manifest.py --check</code> gives <code>all_ok: true</code>.</li></ul><p><strong>Predictions that missed</strong> (recorded): family share (predicted &gt; 40%, observed 27%), test-split size, HumanEval strict count (predicted ≤ 70, observed 74), and MBPP vs MBPP+ agreement (79% vs a predicted 95%).</p><p><strong>Next</strong>: re-score every checkpoint on <code>val_clean80</code> / <code>val_clean50</code> (SQ100); decide on <code>code-py-v2</code> before the next big replication (SQ102); swap in the strict contaminated-ids list after the campaign (SQ101). The ledger rows wait for the ledger redesign (C140–C149 proposals in the folder).</p>"
