   "version": "v0.4.33",
   "title": "K9 FAILS: 41.7% of val is near-duplicated train text",
   "date": "2026-11-22",
   "html": "<p>### K9: train/val near-duplicate leakage (FAIL)</p><p><strong>What</strong>: <code>experiments/checks/k9_leakage.py</code>, registered in v0.4.30 and run as CPU job <code>k9-20261122-181814</code> (200 s). It hashes every 13-gram of the 4,000 val docs (the same docs as K8) against 262.4M train tokens, with a pseudo-val of 4,000 held-out train docs and a shuffled control as baselines.</p><p><strong>Results</strong> (<code>experiments/checks/results/K9.json</code>, card <code>experiments/cards/K9.md</code>, micro devlog <code>experiments/devlogs/K9.md</code>):</p><ul><li><strong>1,667 of 4,000 val docs (41.7%) are near-duplicates of train</strong> (≥ 80% of their 13-grams in train); 70.7% are ≥ 50%. The gate was &lt; 1%: <strong>FAIL</strong>.</li><li>E0 scores <strong>0.101 bits/char on the near-duplicates</strong>. Without them, its full-val figure goes <strong>0.2209 → 0.2779 (+25.8%)</strong>. The gate was &lt; 2%: <strong>FAIL</strong>.</li><li><strong>The pseudo-val overlaps exactly as much</strong> (per-doc mean 0.663 vs 0.665; ≥ 50%: 69.5% vs 70.7%), the shuffled control is 0%, and excluding boilerplate barely changes anything. So the corpus itself is self-duplicated: many LeetCode/MBPP-style problems recur in near-identical variants. It's not a split bug.</li><li>Prediction: 1 hit (val ≈ pseudo-val), 2 misses.</li></ul><p><strong>What it means</strong>: every training-time val number so far (E0, E1, E2, E3, E9, E10, K7) is inflated by near-memorisable docs, and comparisons may be confounded by how well each architecture memorises repeats. K8's \"beats SmolLM2 by more than 2×\" is explained in part.</p><p><strong>Actions</strong>:</p><ul><li>Re-score every checkpoint doc-level on a <strong>clean val</strong> (near-dups removed; A1 builds the list and manifest).</li><li>The Phase 2b primary metric becomes clean-val bits/char plus a memorisation gap. A2 is registering this deviation, dated, before any Phase 2b result.</li><li>A deduplicated <code>code-py-v2</code> is being sized (A1), and the factorial will use deduplicated data (A3).</li><li>Phase 2b keeps running; its checkpoints get re-scored.</li></ul><p><strong>Ledger</strong>: <strong>C94 → REFUTED</strong>; C107 → VERIFIED (val is exact-dedup only); <strong>C131 new, VERIFIED</strong> (the corpus is self-duplicated); C5 and C92 stay PARTIAL, pending the clean-val re-score. The README status line now carries the caveat.</p>"
